// Ranges whose parts do not agree once the loop has been rewritten.
//
// 'each' has no type rules of its own: the parser lowers it into a comparison,
// an increment and possibly a '+=', and it is those that are type checked. That
// is why two of the three diagnostics below are reported at their function's
// closing '}' rather than at the loop -- the nodes the parser synthesizes carry
// the lexer's position at the time the block finished parsing, not the position
// of the range that produced them. The annotations therefore sit on the '}' of
// the function, and the comment above each one says which loop it is really
// about.

// The two bounds are the operands of one comparison, so they must be of one
// type. An i32 lower bound and a u32 upper bound have no '<=' between them, and
// the condition is then untyped for the 'while' that wants a boolean. This one
// is reported where it belongs, on the operator.
fn mismatchedBounds() {
  each i in 0 <= 5u {    //~ ErrorNoCandidate:15 "No method declared by `<=`"
  }                      //~^ ErrorInvType:15 follow-on
}

// Whatever the bounds are, the loop variable is stepped by the increment
// operator, so a type with no '++' cannot be iterated over however well its
// comparison type checks. The '+++' is the post-increment the parser appended
// to the body of the loop just below.
fn notIncrementable() {
  each b in false < true {
  }
}                        //~ ErrorNoMbr:1 "`+++` not found"

// A 'by' step becomes a '+=' on the loop variable, so it has to be a type the
// variable can be added to. An unsigned step does not add to a signed one. The
// '+' is the '+=' the parser appended to the body of the loop just below.
fn mismatchedStep() {
  each i in 0 < 5 by 1u {
  }
}                        //~ ErrorNoCandidate:1 "No method declared by `+`"
